Modern physics routinely extracts unique, testable numbers from expressions that are infinite and formally divergent. Our ability to accurately describe the physics of much of the microscopic world---with some of the predictions of Quantum Field Theory (QFT) experimentally tested to an accuracy of one part in a trillion---often depends on extracting such finite quantities. This raises a basic question: how does the finite emerge from out of the infinite? How can well-defined, finite predictions emerge from divergent sums and integrals? To what extent---by symmetry or dynamics or some other physical criterion---is the unique finite remainder of a divergent sum or integral physically mandated? 




